item.content:encoded:
Most years PostgreSQL ships a new release in September, but this year's release came two months later thanks mainly to the flurry of issues that the PostgreSQL team needed to address as part of the ongoing AI-reported vulnerability apocalypse.This release was definitely worth the wait. My favorite new feature is this one:For applications that send reads to standbys, PostgreSQL 19 adds "read-your-writes" consistency with the new  command. A session on a standby can use  to wait until the standby has replayed the write-ahead log (WAL) up to a specific log sequence number (LSN) before running a query, so the application can read data it just wrote on the primary.In practice, that looks like this:UPDATE movie SET genre = 'Dramatic' WHERE genre = 'Drama';
SELECT pg_current_wal_insert_lsn();
That returns a value like . Store that somewhere (a signed cookie would work) such that on a subsequent request your application can do:WAIT FOR LSN '0/0306EE20' WITH (TIMEOUT '0.1s');
SELECT * FROM movie WHERE genre = 'Drama';
The timeout is optional - if you provide one you'll get an error if the replica you are talking to doesn't catch up within the specified time.I first encountered this pattern in Wikipedia, which has a hand-rolled version of this built against MySQL replication:In addition to this, MediaWiki attempts to ensure that the user sees events occurring on the wiki in chronological order. A few seconds of lag can be tolerated, as long as the user sees a consistent picture from subsequent requests. This is done by saving the primary binlog position in the session, and then at the start of each request, waiting for the replica to catch up to that position before doing any reads from it.

Six months ago, I wrote an SQLite-backed cache to speed up the build system for this site. A static site generator is an ideal candidate for caching, because there are lots of steps that have deterministic behaviour and no side effects. On a site with hundreds of pages, even a few milliseconds per step adds up to a meaningful improvement.That first cache was fairly basic. It supported getting and setting strings, with a namespace for different functions, and not much else: SQLiteCache
cachesetnamespace key value printcachegetnamespace keyThis showed the value of a cache, but it provided cache , not cache . If I wanted to cache a function, I had to write per-function logic for saving values to the cache and retrieving them later. A lot of that code was tricky to test, and broken in subtle ways. I was so unsure of this code that I deleted the cache whenever I built the site for publication.I wanted something better, so last week I wrote funkycache, a new library that encapsulates all of my caching logic, and is much easier to use and test. It can store arbitrary Python objects as both inputs and outputs, and the interface is much simpler for the caller.To cache a function, you create a instance and add a decorator to your function: FunkyCachestorage
@cacheregister str int date tuplestr dateThis is modelled after in the standard library. The output of the expensive function is written to an SQLite database, so it’s available after the Python process exits.The cache tries to be smart about invalidations. If the source file containing changes, the cache for that function is invalidated and will be rebuilt on the next call.You can also specify a dependency on other files and static values, changes to which will also trigger a cache invalidation: FunkyCachestorage
@cacheregister files valuespygments__version__ str int datetime tuplestr datetimeWhen writing funkycache, I wanted to spend a week thinking hard about caching logic, test it thoroughly, then not think about it again for months. You should be able to use funkycache without understanding how it works – but if you’d like to, that’s what I’ll explain in the rest of this post.Cache keys are function arguments, hashed and JSON-encodedThe cache is meant for functions which always produce the same output for a given input, so the inputs are the cache key. When we decorate a function, we can see the arguments passed on each call: @wrapsfunc print args kwargs funcargskwargs wrapperThis doesn’t account for different styles of calling the same function – for example, positional versus keyword arguments, or omitting or overriding default arguments.To get a complete, consistent set of arguments, I use the module: inspectsignaturefunc sigbindargskwargs boundapply_defaults boundarguments This gives me a Python dictionary of the complete set of arguments to the function, which is the same regardless of the calling style.Converting the arguments to JSONPython dictionaries aren’t hashable, so they can’t be used as the keys in dictionaries, nor can they be used as keys in SQLite databases. We need to serialise them as a string or byte sequence.I considered two options for this serialisation: pickle and JSON. In my limited testing, JSON was faster than pickle, so that’s what I chose. Using JSON also gave me more flexibility for speeding up the serialisation.My initial serialisation was a naive call, with a couple of options to ensure consistent ordering and handle types that the standard library doesn’t know how to encode: jsondumpscache_key sort_keys defaultstrWhen I started using funkycache, this encoding step quickly emerged as the slowest part, so I started “reducing” the arguments to make this step faster. This reduction included:Recursively walking the function arguments and hashing large stringsUsing Pydantic to convert Pydantic models and dataclasses into Python objects, rather than doing a string conversionRefactoring my code so each function only takes a minimal set of required dataThese are all lossy transformations, but that’s fine for a cache – we never need to retrieve the original value. It’s theoretically possible for different values to get a colliding hash key, but at my scale it’s unlikely. (A clean build of my site only uses 32,000 keys.)The functions in my static site generator are processing things like the content of articles, so individual function arguments might be kilobytes in size. You can use multi-kilobytes keys with SQLite, but you probably shouldn’t – you take a performance hit for larger keys.I wrote a small benchmark to compare three approaches: storing the JSON-encoded arguments directly, hashing them with MD5, and hashing them with XXH64. The results are a wash for small key sizes, but for larger keys XXH64 is significantly faster, and MD5 is slower than storing the keys directly in SQLite:Based on this benchmark, funkycache hashes the JSON cache key with XXH64 before inserting it into SQLite.The xxHash class of hashing algorithms was a pleasant discovery for me during this project. They are explicitly designed to be fast, not cryptographically secure. Previously I’d used MD5 for this purpose, because it’s widely available and I conflated “weak” with “fast”. Actually running a benchmark revealed my mistake.Cache values are JSON-encodedSerialising cache keys can be a one-way operation, but cached values need to be retrievable. For this, I’m using the module to get the return type from the cached function, then handing the serialisation to Pydantic. inspectsignature sigret_annotation
TypeAdapterreturn_type adapterdumper_jsonvalue adaptervalidate_jsonstored_valueI use type hints and type annotations for all of my Python, so this information is guaranteed to be present. This wouldn’t work with untyped Python, but it’s good enough for me.The alternative is using pickle, which can store arbitrary Python objects. I wouldn’t have to worry about the security risks because I’d only ever be pickling and unpickling trusted data, but I continued with JSON because I’m already using it for cache keys, and it’s easier to debug.One of the functions I wanted to cache is a lazy generator, which was a little trickier.Using will cache the generator , so every call gets the same generator instance, and they all step together. This strikes me as potentially confusing:
@functoolscache range i even_numbers even_numbers printnextn1 printnextn1 printnextn2funkycache takes a different approach: it materialises the generator into a list, and stores that list. When it’s retrieved, the list is converted back into a generator by wrapping it with .To protect against unbounded memory use from a large or infinite generator, there’s a soft memory budget. If the materialised list exceeds this budget, I stop gathering the output in the list and don’t save anything to the cache. The list and any subsequently generated items are passed directly from the original function to the caller.Some of the functions in my static site generator depend on the contents of template files. When those files change, I want to invalidate the cache and rebuild the pages that use those templates.When you register a function with funkycache, you can specify a list of files or folders that it depends on:@cacheregisterfiles str dictstr Any strfunkycache will read the contents of each of those files, and include them in the cache key. When a file changes, it re-evaluates the list of files and decides whether to keep or invalidate the cache.I decided not to put any file change tracking in funkycache itself. I already have code for that, and I wanted to keep this project small and focused.Tracking dependent valuesA function’s cache can also be based on static values. I use this to include the version of important libraries, so the cache gets invalidated when I update the library. Here’s a short example:
@cacheregistervaluespygments__version__ str str strReactive programming is a bit like using a spreadsheet. You have input cells where you enter data, and other cells that read that data and automatically update when the inputs change. Those cells can be inputs to other cells, and so on.Imagine building a static site: your Markdown files act as input cells, and your rendered HTML pages are the dependent cells. Your tasks form a directed acyclic graph (DAG), and updates propagate through the graph.For example, in the diagram above, when changes, the system knows it needs to rebuild and ripples that update to . But because there’s been no change in their inputs, the tasks to render articles 2 and 3 don’t run at all.When done correctly, this can be a very efficient style of programming, because steps only ever run if there’s been a change in their inputs.There are tools that will do this for you, like Apache Airflow, but I wanted to build my own. My website is a sandbox where I like building things myself as a learning exercise, rather than using somebody else’s library.I spent several weeks trying to design an API, but I couldn’t come up with something satisfactory. However, I experimented with a decorator-based API, and that led me to build funkycache, which delivers a lot of the benefits with less fo the complexity.Building my website with a warm cache now takes about half a second, and a significant chunk of that time is spent traversing the file system for Markdown files. I have ideas for speeding up that step as well, but that’s a task for another day.funkycache is only a few hundred lines of code, including tests. If this problem sounds interesting, I encourage you to read it, even if it’s not a library you expect to use.

Google join Jev and OpenAI in the decision model category with "Gemini Decision 1". This new model API is almost identical to Jev: it supports the same three types of questions (yes/no, choice, and rubric-scoring) and even reuses Jev's term "Noul" for a yes/no question.It's priced the same as the GPT-6 Luna decision model: 10 cents per million input tokens, and output is free. Jev remains the cheapest of the three, at 4.2 cents per million.Jev is text (and JSON) only. OpenAI can handle text and images. Gemini Decision 1 is fully multi-model: it covers text, images, audio, video, and PDFs.dragon: Does this video have a dragon in it?
robot: Does this video have a robot in it?
france: Is this video set in France?
usa: Is this video set in the USA?
Jev and OpenAI both return 1.0 or 0.0 for yes/no questions, but Gemini insists on being a little less confident than that:{
